/**
	Reads the entire content of a file and returns it as a string.
	string file_get_contents(string filename)
	## Arguments:
		* string filename: the name of the file to read.
	## Returns:
		The contents of the file on success, an empty string on failure.
	## Remarks:
		This is a convenience function. If you wish to access a file with more control, see the file datastream class.
		On Android and on Apple platforms, this function finds a file by its name differently from the other filesystem functions, which take a relative name from the current working directory (on Android, the root of the device's filesystem, as NVGT doesn't change it there):
		* On Android, a relative name is looked up in the app's internal storage (DIRECTORY_APPDATA) first and then among the assets packed into the app. A file written there with file_put_contents and a relative name is therefore not found by file_exists or a file object given the same name; build the full path from DIRECTORY_APPDATA when a file is used with both.
		* On macOS and iOS, a file of that name inside the app's resources (its Resources folder, or the .app on iOS) is read if there is one, whether the name is relative or not, and otherwise the name is used as given. In a compiled app the working directory is that folder already, so this only matters for a name that is found in both places.
		Be careful to avoid reading files that are too large with this function, as the entire file will be in memory in order to return it as a continguous string.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	string filename = input_box("Filename", "Enter the name of a file to read.", "");
	string contents = file_get_contents(filename);
	if (contents == "")
		alert("Example", "Either the file was not found, or it contained no text.");
	else {
		clipboard_set_text(contents);
		alert("Example", "The contents of the file is now on your clipboard.");
	}
}
